[04:56 - 05:04] SPEAKER_00: "Now, as you can tell, the main occupations of this time, the Paleolithic era, are largely gone."
[05:05 - 05:08] SPEAKER_00: "Hunters, gatherers, toolmakers,"
[05:08 - 05:11] SPEAKER_00: "But they didn't disappear overnight."
[05:11 - 05:15] SPEAKER_00: "Instead, they were subdivided into lots of other more specialized jobs."
[05:17 - 05:18] SPEAKER_00: "So why don't we move forward?"
[05:20 - 05:22] SPEAKER_00: "to the Neolithic era."
[05:22 - 05:33] SPEAKER_00: "Now here, humans have mastered a few new things, farming, keeping livestock, and I'm gonna zoom through this, but this was a big transformation."
[05:34 - 05:43] SPEAKER_00: "Actually, the invention of these things allowed us to spend more time doing what we consider creative work and less time on pure survival and subsistence."
[05:43 - 05:45] SPEAKER_00: "And this opened up a lot of new jobs."
[05:45 - 05:56] SPEAKER_00: "You had artisans like weavers, you had farmers, of course, potters, construction laborers, and these jobs, too, largely all gone."
